    q: "What are the components and points for the historic CHADS₂ score, and how is it used to determine anticoagulation in atrial fibrillation?",
    a: "<b>CHADS₂ Score Components:</b><ul><li><b>C - Congestive heart failure:</b> 1 point</li><li><b>H - Hypertension:</b> 1 point</li><li><b>A - Age &ge; 75 years:</b> 1 point</li><li><b>D - Diabetes mellitus:</b> 1 point</li><li><b>S₂ - Stroke or TIA history:</b> 2 points</li></ul><br><b>Management Algorithm:</b><ul><li><b>Score 0:</b> Low risk. Aspirin or no therapy.</li><li><b>Score 1:</b> Intermediate risk. Consider aspirin or oral anticoagulation.</li><li><b>Score &ge; 2:</b> High risk. <b>Oral anticoagulation</b> (e.g., warfarin or DOAC) is indicated.</li></ul><b>Clinical pearl:</b> The <b>CHA₂DS₂-VASc</b> score has largely replaced CHADS₂ because it is better at identifying truly \"low risk\" patients who do not require anticoagulation."
