    q: "What is the diagnostic workup algorithm for suspected diabetes insipidus (DI) and how do you differentiate central vs. nephrogenic DI?",
    a: "<b>Diabetes Insipidus (DI) Workup Algorithm:</b><ol><li>**Confirm polyuria:** Document 24-hour urine output **&gt; 3 L/day** with low urine osmolarity (**&lt; 300 mOsm/kg**) in the presence of hypernatremia or high normal serum sodium.</li><li>**Water Deprivation Test:** Deprive patient of water and monitor weight, serum sodium, and urine osmolarity hourly.</li><ul><li>If urine osmolarity rises **&gt; 600 mOsm/kg**: Diagnosis is **primary polydipsia**.</li><li>If urine osmolarity remains **&lt; 300 mOsm/kg** despite serum osmolarity &gt; 295 or sodium &gt; 145: Confirm **DI** and proceed to step 3.</li></ul><li>**Desmopressin (dDAVP) Stimulation:** Administer exogenous desmopressin and measure urine osmolarity:</li><ul><li>**Central DI:** Urine osmolarity **increases by &gt; 50%**. Treat with **desmopressin**.</li><li>**Nephrogenic DI:** Urine osmolarity **increases by &lt; 10%** (no significant response). Treat with **thiazide diuretics**, **amiloride**, and dietary sodium restriction.</li></ul></ol>"
